{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9xVyt7sgtJsaUTsvtkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `Hijack()` so it fails only after body bytes have been written. Before, it also failed when only the headers had been flushed. The change is in your working tree and not committed.\n\n**Changed:**\n- `response_writer.go`: the check is now `w.size > 0` instead of `w.Written()`. Hijacking is allowed when nothing has been written (`size == -1`) or when only headers were flushed (`size == 0`). This matters for websocket libraries, which send the 101 status and then hijack. I added a short comment explaining why.\n- `response_writer_test.go`: new case \"hijack after only header write should succeed\". It calls `WriteHeader(101)` and `WriteHeaderNow()`, then hijacks.\n\n**Verified:**\n- The new test failed before the fix with `gin: response already written`.\n- `go test ./...` passes in every package, and `go vet` and `gofmt` are clean.\n- With `response_writer.go` reverted, the new test fails again.\n- The existing case \"hijack after write should fail\" still passes, so hijacking after body data is still rejected.\n\n**Behaviour change:**\n- `Hijack()` after `WriteHeaderNow()` now succeeds instead of returning an error.\n- `Write([]byte{})` leaves the size at 0, so a hijack after an empty write is also allowed. No body bytes have been sent at that point, so I think that's acceptable.\n\n**Not done:** HEAD is detached. Tell me if you want a commit (subject: `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle`) and which branch to put it on."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":703,"cache_read_input_tokens":38831,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":nul